ives the decoded struct and returns any Serialize value. A structured output (RunOptions.output = Some(output_type::<T>())) is validated against T's schema and comes back as JSON in result.output; decode it with serde_json::from_value::<T>."],[2530,"The client contract, in all sixteen pairings. A contract host in each language (sdks/conformance/contract/ts_host.mjs, python_host.py, the Go one in sdks/go/internal/contracthost and the Rust examples/contract_host.rs) builds an agent from a JSON config and serves it on a free port (POST /serve, /serve-agents, /close). TypeScript's own packages/server/src/client-contract.test.ts runs unmodified against a host (a vitest setup redirects createAgent and serve to it), and its Python port sdks/python/tests/server/test_client_contract.py does the same, as do the Go and Rust ports:"],[2131,"The client contract, in all sixteen pairings. A contract host in each language (sdks/conformance/contract/ts_host.mjs, python_host.py, the Go one in sdks/go/internal/contracthost and the Rust examples/contract_host.rs) builds an agent from a JSON config and serves it on a free port (POST /serve, /serve-agents, /close). TypeScript's packages/server/src/client-contract.test.ts runs unmodified against any host, and so do its Python, Go and Rust ports:"],[2357,"The client contract, in all sixteen pairings. A contract host in each language (sdks/conformance/contract/ts_host.mjs, python_host.py, the Go one in sdks/go/internal/contracthost, and sdks/rust/examples/contract_host.rs) builds an agent from a JSON config and serves it on a free port (POST /serve, /serve-agents, /close). TypeScript's packages/server/src/client-contract.test.ts runs unmodified against any host, and so do its Python, Go and Rust ports (the Rust one is sdks/rust/tests/contract):"],[2423,"The client is async only. agentkit.client replaced the stdlib scaffold that lived in clients/python."],[2363,"The config dict keeps TypeScript's keys"],[1945,"The config is a struct named after TypeScript's keys"],[2137,"The config is a struct named after TypeScript's keys"],[1053,"The context a rule sees: messages, lastUser (context a runtime injects mid-turn is not counted as the user), toolResults (this turn), results (the same, decoded: { name, value } with JSON parsed and compacted results unwrapped), tools (names offered), system, and match (the regex result)."],[2230,"The crate is published as headless-agentkit and imported as agentkit (the crate named agentkit on crates.io is an unrelated project). Releases are tagged sdks/rust/vX.Y.Z; the version tracks @agentkit/core's."],[2657,"the current plan with step statuses, or undefined"],[1171,"The daemon. A long-lived process that owns the runtime, mounts handleSessionRoutes behind your auth, and maps users to sessions through its scope callback."],[2133,"The dashboards, unchanged. Two pages built for the TypeScript server run against the Go one with no edits:"],[2532,"The dashboards, unchanged. Two pages built for the TypeScript server run against the Python one (and the Go and Rust ones) with no edits:"],[2359,"The dashboards, unchanged. Two pages built for the TypeScript server run against the Rust one with no edits:"],[2232,"The default build runs the mock provider, the in-memory stores and SQLite (bundled, no system library). Everything else is a Cargo feature, so a program that never asks for a backend does not link its driver:"],[1847,"The difference between the two mechanisms matters. A tools/pre-execute listener is policy — reorderable, overridable, and the right home for \"ask the user\". A guard is an invariant — it only ever adds refusals, and a derived registry inherits it, so moving into a realm cannot escape it."],[1894,"The exec handle"],[1749,"the execution world is one row; the tests swap it for an in-memory one and every tool follows"],[1200,"The first is static: the resolved row list after patches, before anything runs. The second is live: the fiber tree, including any plugin sti